            <div xml:id="d281">

                <pb facs="0528" n="408" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg408"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 Steering Committee/219: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Delegate (Wilson) to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-12-05T22:00:00+01:00">December 5,
                            1932—10 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 5—8:33 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>473. From Davis. Today Matsudaira gave me the text of the Japanese navy proposal.
                    It is a closely knit plan involving qualitative <pb facs="0529" n="409"
                        xml:id="pg409"/> restrictions in all categories; quantitative restrictions
                    and consequently ratios in the so-called offensive categories of capital ships,
                    aircraft carriers and heavy cruisers; “uniform tonnage” for five principle
                    powers in defensive categories of light cruisers, destroyers and submarines; and
                    a system of general and special agreements based upon regional grouping. A study
                    of full text is necessary for complete understanding. I presume that you may
                    have been presented with a copy. If not shall I cable full text? [Davis.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
